  <description akvo:type="4" type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">BBB Bolivia</narrative>
  </description>
  <description akvo:type="6" type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The local partner of the DTZ program in Bolivia is the Munasim Kullakita Foundation (FMK), which implements the actions of the program. This non-profit organization has 12 years of experience in the care and prevention of CSEC. Its scope is national and it has offices in three departments of the country: La Paz, Santa Cruz and Cochabamba. FMK is member of ECPAT and the main technical reference before the State on CSEC issues, currently the president of the Bolivian Network to Fight Trafficking and Smuggling of Persons and Migrants, an entity that represents civil society in the Plurinational Council of Trafficking and Traffic, the main executor of the Bolivian State&apos;s anti-trafficking policy.
The consortium of institutions that support the implementation of actions in Bolivia are Icco Cooperation and International Defense of Children - ECPAT the Netherlands, who have extensive and recognized experience in different regions of the world in addressing CSEC and have become a benchmark in care and protection of children. Icco Cooperation will support all processes at the level of care for children, Government and Community and ECPAT Holland will work with the private sector and protection of children in travel and tourism.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description akvo:type="5" type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Work will be done on consolidating the processes already established during the DTZ program (2016-2020), taking advantage of the knowledge and experience acquired from the FMK to strengthen processes initiated before the pandemic crisis, such as the care and protection of victims of CSEC and at risk and the development of child and youth leaders who empower themselves of their rights have been assuming an active role in their communities in preventing CSEC</narrative>
  </description>
